# Post-fork copyright attribution

All code **written after the fork** is attributed to **QuickFAST contributors** — never to Object
Computing, Inc. (OCI). OCI holds copyright only on the pre-fork upstream code it actually wrote.

This applies to **new files and to pre-fork files you modify**. Editing an upstream file adds
post-fork content to it, so that file must carry the contributors line too.

The contributors line is always exactly:

```
Copyright (c) 2026, QuickFAST contributors.
```

The year stays `2026` (the fork year). Do not bump it, and do not grow it into a range or list when
touching a file again later.

## New files

Three lines, at the very top:

```cpp
// Copyright (c) 2026, QuickFAST contributors.
// All rights reserved.
// See the file license.txt for licensing information.
```

Use the comment syntax of the file's language (`#` for CMake/Python/shell, `<!-- -->` for HTML/XML
project files), but keep the three lines and the exact wording, including the comma and trailing
period. Below a `#!` shebang, not above it. Leave a blank line after the header when the next line
is not itself blank.

## Modified pre-fork files

Leave the OCI line **byte-for-byte as it is** and add the contributors line directly below it, so
the holders read in chronological order. Everything else in the header stays put.

```cpp
// ✅ upstream file, modified after the fork
// Copyright (c) 2009, Object Computing, Inc.
// Copyright (c) 2026, QuickFAST contributors.
// All rights reserved.
// See the file license.txt for licensing information.
```

This holds for every OCI variant in the tree (`2009`, `2009, 2010`, `2009, 2010, 2011`, `2011`).
Match the comment prefix already on the OCI line. Add the line once — never a second copy.

A file relocated from upstream still counts as pre-fork content: if it carries an OCI header, treat
it as a modified pre-fork file, whatever git reports about the rename.

## Which files carry a header

Headers go on source and build files: `*.cpp`, `*.h`, `*.cmake`, `CMakeLists.txt`, `*.py`, `*.sh`,
`*.csproj`.

Do **not** add one to generated or non-code files: `src/Doxyfile`, `*.md`, `.gitignore`,
`.cursor/rules/*.mdc`, test resources (`*.xml`, `*.bin`, `*.pcap`, `*.pcapng`).

## Hard rules

- Do **not** write `Copyright (c) 2026, Object Computing, Inc.` or any other year paired with OCI on
  a file created after the fork.
- Do **not** ship a bare `// Copyright (c) 2026` with no holder.
- Do **not** reword, reformat, re-year or remove an existing pre-fork OCI line. Adding the
  contributors line below it is the only change that header normally takes. Correcting an outright
  spelling error in the word `Copyright` itself is the one exception, and the tree is clean of those.
- Do **not** add our copyright to vendored third-party files. `cmake/PVS-Studio.cmake` is
  PVS-Studio LLC's; leave it alone. Anything else arriving with a foreign copyright is the same.
- When you notice a post-fork file carrying only an OCI header, fix it to the contributors line.
- Preserve each file's existing line endings and encoding when inserting a header. Repo line endings
  are mixed; a header edit must not turn into a whole-file CRLF/LF churn.

```cpp
// ❌ new file, wrong holder
// Copyright (c) 2026 Object Computing, Inc.

// ❌ no holder
// Copyright (c) 2026

// ❌ modified upstream file, OCI attribution dropped
// Copyright (c) 2026, QuickFAST contributors.

// ✅
// Copyright (c) 2026, QuickFAST contributors.
```
